{"name":"Breezeway","hex":"#d6dbc0","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"breezeway","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A covered walkway, with open sides, that connects two buildings.","The space between two groups of rowhouses in the middle of a city block."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/breezeway"}],"entries":[{"title":"Breezeway","blocks":[{"text":"A breezeway is an architectural feature similar to a hallway that allows the passage of a breeze between structures to accommodate high winds, allow aeration, or provide aesthetic design variation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Mainstreetcomplex1.JPG/330px-Mainstreetcomplex1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breezeway","wikidata":"Q4960350"},{"title":"Breezeway (car)","blocks":[{"text":"Breezeway is a term for a vertical or reverse-slanted, power-operated retractable rear window on sedans. Intended to provide through ventilation. It first appeared on the 1957-1958 Mercury Turnpike Cruisers and the 1958–1960 Continental Mark III, IV, & V. After a break it reappeared on some 1963–1968 full-size Mercurys, including some Park Lanes, Montclairs, and Montereys."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Mercury_Breezeway_roof_detail.jpg/330px-Mercury_Breezeway_roof_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breezeway_(car)","wikidata":"Q28454983"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/1958_Lincoln_Continental_Mark_III_Convertible_%2829742980866%29.jpg/500px-1958_Lincoln_Continental_Mark_III_Convertible_%2829742980866%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/1958_Lincoln_Continental_Mark_III_Convertible_%2829742980866%29.jpg/1280p